  VERDICT FOR THIS PAIR: DISCRIMINATING. This is this run's answer to the
  question the A/B exists to answer - is Opengrep's taint engine active on
  Spark's own Scala, and does the taint setting demonstrably change what it
  reports? One engine, one pinned rule, one file, and --taint-intrafile as the
  only analysis difference: 2 finding(s) at line(s) 828, 834, every one of
  them carrying a 5-step dataflow trace, with the flag; 0 finding(s) without
  it, its SARIF results array empty. Enabled-in-config is not proof (AAP
  0.1.3) and this pair does not rest on configuration: it rests on the
  contrast between two invocations whose argv differs by one analysis token.

  THE CONTRAST IS PRODUCED BY FLOW THAT CROSSES METHOD BOUNDARIES, which is
  precisely what --taint-intrafile supplies and what the engine's default
  intraprocedural analysis cannot see. Both traced findings carry the same
  five-step thread flow, quoted verbatim from the ON arm's own SARIF:

      Source: 'predicates' @ 'sql/hive/src/main/scala/org/apache/spark/sql/hive/client/HiveShim.scala:351'
      Call: 'convertFilters' @ 'sql/hive/src/main/scala/org/apache/spark/sql/hive/client/HiveShim.scala:351'
      Propagator : 'filters' @ 'sql/hive/src/main/scala/org/apache/spark/sql/hive/client/HiveShim.scala:645'
      Propagator : 'flatMap' @ 'sql/hive/src/main/scala/org/apache/spark/sql/hive/client/HiveShim.scala:861'
      Sink: 's"$name like "' @ 'sql/hive/src/main/scala/org/apache/spark/sql/hive/client/HiveShim.scala:828'

  Reading those five steps against the pinned source: 'predicates' is a
  parameter of getPartitionsByFilter, whose def is at HiveShim.scala:344, and
  the source is its use at line 351; the call at line 351 enters
  convertFilters, a DIFFERENT method whose def is at line 645, where the
  tainted value arrives as the parameter 'filters'; the flatMap at line 861
  carries it into the local method convert, whose def is at line 780; and the
  sink is the interpolated SQL string inside that method at line 828 (and at
  834 for the second finding). The flow therefore crosses three method
  boundaries - getPartitionsByFilter (344) -> convertFilters (645) -> convert
  (780) - inside the one file, which is exactly the propagation
  --taint-intrafile enables and its absence removes.

  THE HONEST LIMITATION OF THE OFF ARM, stated so the contrast is not read as
  more than it is: 'off' means INTRAPROCEDURAL TAINT, not no taint. Opengrep
  1.27.1 exposes no option that disables taint analysis at all - APPENDIX C
  carries this session's own measurement of the engine's whole option surface
  and both taint-named options verbatim - so a truly taint-free arm is not
  constructible at this pin. What the pair proves is that the taint setting
  changes what the engine reports on this code, by 2 traced findings against
  0.

