APPENDIX C - engine capability: the taint options, measured here
================================================================================

  Measured by this session against the engine named in the identity block. The
  figures below are why the OFF arm of either pair means 'intraprocedural
  taint' rather than 'no taint': the engine exposes exactly two options whose
  name mentions taint and neither of them disables taint analysis.

$ opengrep scan --help | grep -c "^ \+--"
107

$ COLUMNS=80 opengrep scan --help | grep -c "^ \+--"
107

$ opengrep scan --help | wc -l
558

$ opengrep scan --help | grep -nE "^ +--(no-)?[a-z0-9-]*taint[a-z0-9-]*$"
192:       --guarded-taint-signatures
445:       --taint-intrafile

$ opengrep scan --help | grep -ni "taint"
192:       --guarded-taint-signatures
193:           Attach branch-condition guards to taint effects and evaluate them
445:       --taint-intrafile
446:           Enable intra-file inter-procedural taint analysis. Supported

$ opengrep scan --help | grep -A5 -E "^ +--taint-intrafile$"
       --taint-intrafile
           Enable intra-file inter-procedural taint analysis. Supported
           languages: Apex, C, Clojure, C#, C++, Dart, Elixir, Go, Java,
           JavaScript, Julia, Kotlin, Lua, Python, Ruby, Rust, Scala, Swift,
           TypeScript, Visual Basic. Other languages will fall back to
           intraprocedural analysis only.

$ opengrep scan --help | grep -A4 -E "^ +--guarded-taint-signatures$"
       --guarded-taint-signatures
           Attach branch-condition guards to taint effects and evaluate them
           at call sites, dropping effects whose guard is false. Without this
           flag only Clojure keeps the arity guards that implement
           multi-arity dispatch. REQUIRES --experimental

$ opengrep scan --help | grep -cE "^ {7}(-[a-zA-Z]|--)"
110

$ opengrep scan --help | grep -nE "^ {7}-[a-zA-Z]"
25:       -a, --autofix
53:       -d, --dump-command-for-core
106:       -e VAL, --pattern=VAL
149:       -f VAL, -c VAL, --config=VAL (absent SEMGREP_RULES env)
236:       -j INT, --jobs=INT (absent=16)
252:       -l VAL, --lang=VAL
332:       -o VAL, --output=VAL
379:       -q, --quiet
487:       -v, --verbose

$ opengrep scan --help | grep -nE "^ {11}--"   # wrapped description lines that merely begin with --
162:           --config p/python --config myrules/myrule.yaml` See
168:           --experimental
224:           --incremental-output
385:           --pro. Incompatible with --project-root. Note this requires an
435:           --experimental
520:           --x-ls. THIS OPTION IS NOT PART OF THE SEMGREP API AND MAY CHANGE

  Reading those measurements. `grep -c "^ \+--"` returns 107 at the default
  width and at COLUMNS=80, over a help text of 558 lines, so terminal width
  does not move the figure; but 107 is not the option count. 110 declarations
  sit at 7-space indent, of which 9 are declared with a short alias first (the
  nine lines listed above, at help lines 25, 53, 106, 149, 236, 252, 332, 379
  and 487) and so can never match a pattern anchored on '--'; and 6 of the 107
  matches are wrapped description continuation lines at 11-space indent that
  merely begin with '--' (help lines 162, 168, 224, 385, 435 and 520). The
  engine's true long-option surface is 110. Of those 110, exactly two are
  taint-named: --taint-intrafile ENABLES intra-file inter-procedural taint and
  its own help text says the fallback is 'intraprocedural analysis only'
  rather than no analysis, and --guarded-taint-signatures attaches
  branch-condition guards to taint effects and requires --experimental. A
  case-insensitive search of the whole help text for 'taint' matches nothing
  but those two names and their own descriptions. There is no --no-taint,
  --disable-taint, --taint-off or equivalent at this version, so a truly
  taint-free arm is not constructible and neither pair claims one.

